Qwen3-8B       & Qwen    & 8.2B & 0.692 & [.674, .708] & 0.714 \\
Qwen3-0.6B     & Qwen    & 0.6B & 0.691 & [.673, .709] & 0.780 \\
Mistral-7B     & Mistral & 7.0B & 0.633 & [.617, .648] & 0.682 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{Average (8-gen LOGO)}} & \textbf{0.694} & [.688, .700] & \textbf{0.762} \\
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textbf{LOFO (family-level)}} & 0.686 & --- & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\vspace{-1mm}
\subsection{Differential Depth Response}
\label{sec:results-differential}

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_baseline_comparison.pdf}
\caption{LOGO binary F1 vs.\ inference cost in ms/text (8-gen pooled). The 7-feature ensemble (one 410M reference model) matches multi-detector ensembles (two models, 1.4B total) at ${\sim}2.4\times$ lower cost; individual detectors vary widely when repurposed for depth. Inference costs measured on a single NVIDIA RTX 5090.}
\label{fig:baseline-ci}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Formal definition.}
For feature $f$ measured across depths $d \in \{0,1,2{+}\}$, we define its \emph{depth sensitivity} as $\text{DDR}(f) = |\rho(\text{depth}, f)|$, where $\rho$ is Spearman correlation over all per-text measurements.
A feature is classified as \emph{drifting} if Cohen's $d > 0.50$ between adjacent depths, or \emph{stationary} if $d < 0.15$; these thresholds are reference anchors, not definitive boundaries---the two-cluster structure is robust across $\varepsilon \in [0.10, 0.75]$, supported by unsupervised $k$-means (silhouette\,=\,0.660, $p{=}0.030$), exhaustive enumeration of all 35 splits (this partition ranks first), and independent replication in all eight generators (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}); crucially, each generator's data is fully independent, making per-generator split analysis an 8-fold independent validation of the partition rather than same-data circularity.
Recursive inputs grow increasingly self-typical~\citep{markovian_chains}, predicting location drift with shape invariance (confirmed across nucleus, greedy, and top-$k$ strategies; Appendix~\ref{app:decoding-strategy}).

Figure~\ref{fig:ddr-trajectory} reveals the dichotomy: central-tendency features (entropy mean, TTR, hapax ratio) decline monotonically with depth, while shape features (entropy max, tail decay, entropy min) remain flat.
The partition is binary ($k{=}2$; silhouette\,=\,0.660), with entropy std as a sole \emph{intermediate} member ($d{=}0.55$, $|\rho|{=}0.16$), distinct from three near-stationary features ($d < 0.10$; Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}).

\begin{figure}[t!]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_differential_response.pdf}
\caption{Differential depth response (DDR). Diamonds: cross-generator means; dots: per-generator values. Drifting features (blue; $|\rho| > 0.60$, $d{=}0.76$--$1.17$) show strong monotonic depth sensitivity; non-drifting features remain stable. Entropy std (reference-model NLL; wine; $d{=}0.55$) is intermediate; near-stationary features (amber; $d \leq 0.10$) show negligible drift. Marker shapes encode feature identity for grayscale accessibility.}
\label{fig:feature-drift}
\end{figure}

Pooled Spearman correlations corroborate the split: drifting features show $|\rho| > 0.60$ (hapax ratio $-0.64$, TTR $-0.63$, entropy mean $-0.60$) vs.\ $|\rho| \leq 0.16$ for non-drifting---a six-fold magnitude gap defining the DDR partition (Figure~\ref{fig:feature-drift}).

\paragraph{Quantification.}
Cohen's $d$ \cite{cohen1988statistical} shows a ${\geq}7{\times}$ gap between drifting ($d{=}0.76$--$1.17$) and near-stationary ($d \leq 0.08$); KS, Glass's $\Delta$, Cliff's $\delta$ all corroborate (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}).
Entropy min and tail decay are \emph{boundary features}: entropy min is near-stationary for gen-1/gen-2+ ($d{=}0.016$) but sensitive at gen-0/gen-1; tail decay passes TOST for only 4/8 generators ($|d|{=}0.10$--$0.22$).
\paragraph{Validation.}
Each hold-out generator independently recovers the identical partition (silhouette 0.505--0.760; Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-validation}); TOST confirms near-stationarity for entropy min (8/8) and entropy max (6/8); permutation testing confirms the split as non-random ($p{=}0.030$); $\Delta|\rho| \leq 0.05$ across splits; disjoint generator-pool splits, heteroscedasticity robustness, and per-feature convergence dynamics appear in Appendices~\ref{app:holdout-split}--\ref{app:convergence}.

\paragraph{Feature complementarity.}
The full 7-feature set (F1\,=\,0.773, 10-fold stratified CV, complementing the LOGO protocol in Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}) outperforms surface-only (0.688) and reference-only (0.713; McNemar $p \approx 0$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablation-table}).
The collinear drifting triplet captures ${\sim}$1 effective dimension (PCA PC1\,=\,92.3\%; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-feature-correlation}); group ablation confirms complementarity for depth profiling: 3-class F1 gains $+$5.3\,pp when non-drifting features are added (drifting-only 0.716, all-7 0.769), while binary detection is already saturated by location drift alone (drifting-only binary F1\,=\,0.697 vs.\ all-7\,=\,0.700; Appendix~\ref{app:ablation-table})---shape stability contributes to depth \emph{grading}, not detection.
TTR and hapax ratio ($r = 0.97$) are retained for marginal LOFO gains \cite{strobl_conditional_2008} (Appendix~\ref{app:lofo}).

\paragraph{Cross-family reference models.}
Alternative references (OLMo-2-1B, Qwen3-0.6B) yield F1 spread of only 0.013 (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-ref}); same-family proximity may inflate Pythia F1 by up to 1.3\,pp, but the DDR partition is preserved.

\paragraph{Temperature and prompt-type sensitivity.}
All four tested generators degrade at $t{=}0.9$ ($-$9.3 to $-$26.0\,pp binary F1; Appendix~\ref{app:sampling-sensitivity}); continuation chains show the strongest drift (F1\,=\,0.781; Appendix~\ref{app:prompt-type}).
The DDR partition hierarchy is preserved across temperatures (Appendix~\ref{app:ddr-temp-stability}).


\vspace{-1mm}
\subsection{Comparison with Detection Baselines}
\label{sec:results-baselines}

We next ask whether existing detection methods---designed for binary real-vs-synthetic classification---incidentally capture depth structure, repurposing four detection scores as ordinal depth predictors under identical XGBoost and LOGO conditions (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}).
The strongest baseline is a 4-detector + surface feature ensemble (binary F1\,=\,0.708), which pools four detection scores with TTR and hapax ratio; among individual detectors, Avg-LogProb provides the most meaningful single-feature comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\caption{Depth classification (8-gen LOGO, pooled evaluation; pooled F1 is slightly higher than per-generator macro F1 in Table~\ref{tab:logo-results} because larger test folds from high-performing generators contribute proportionally more). Identical XGBoost configuration. 7-feature: one 410M model (905\,MB); 4-det ensembles: two models, 1.4B params (2{,}868\,MB). Individual detection scores (N/A) share the reference-model forward pass; per-method timing is not separately profiled. XGBoost uses 7 handcrafted scalar features (one 410M reference); QLoRA (\S\ref{sec:results-classification}) uses the full token sequence---an information-asymmetric ceiling, not a fair comparison.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Feat.} & \textbf{3-class F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{ms/text} \\
\midrule
4-det.\ + surface     & 6 & \textbf{0.795} & \textbf{0.708} & 22.8 \\
4-detector ens.\      & 4 & 0.785 & 0.700 & 22.8 \\
7-feature (ours)      & 7 & 0.774 & 0.705 & \textbf{9.7} \\
\midrule
Avg-LogProb           & 1 & 0.707 & 0.673 & --- \\
FastDetectGPT         & 1 & 0.660 & 0.569 & --- \\
LD-Score              & 1 & 0.630 & 0.675 & --- \\
Binoculars            & 1 & 0.411 & 0.539 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

The 7-feature ensemble matches the 4-detector+surface ensemble on binary F1 (0.705 vs.\ 0.708; Figure~\ref{fig:baseline-ci}) at ${\sim}2.4\times$ lower cost, though the latter leads by 2.1\,pp on 3-class F1 (0.795 vs.\ 0.774); both surpass individual detectors by 3.0--16.6\,pp.
DDR's contribution is the decomposition into location-sensitive and shape-stable components (\S\ref{sec:discussion}), achieving comparable depth discrimination with a single 410M reference model; ordinal-specific methods (CORAL, Frank-Hall) yield comparable F1 (Appendix~\ref{app:classifier}).
Concurrent iterative-generation studies use different chain operators (back-translation, rephrasing) and target different phenomena (factuality, convergence dynamics), precluding direct inclusion as depth classification baselines (Appendix~\ref{app:iterative-comparison}).

